<div class="post-content">
  <p>
    In soft-goods product design, matching thread type and weight to fabric and use is as important as choosing the fabric itself.
    A weak or wrong-size thread it will fail, look funky (in a bad way), or both. So let's dive into how to make good thread choices.
  </p>
  <p>There's a few things to think about when choosing thread:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Weight: The weight, or thickness, of the thread. Thread weights are numbered. This gets complex but with a little bit of experience you'll get the hang of it. number (e.g. 70 in Mara 70) indicates the weight of the thread per 1,000 meters.</li>
    <li>Material: Thread material, such as polyester, cotton, or polyester/cotton blends. There are also a lot of recycled thread materials available.</li>
    <li>Spool Type & Size: Thread often comes wound on "spools." Bigger spools called "cones."</li>
    <li>Color: The most fun part! Thread color can really make a difference in your product's appearance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="thread-weight-numbers">Thread weights can feel confusing</h2>
  <p>
    The first thing to know is that there are a few different systems for thread size numbers. Thread is one of the oldest and most universally used tools in the world.
    So there have been a LOT of ways people have tried to share a threads thickness. Here's a list of some of the most common systems:
    <ul>
      <li>Weight (wt): Bigger numbers = thinner thread. Why? wt is calculated by how many kilometers of thread weigh one kilogram.</li>
      <li>Gütermann system: The Gütermann company uses their own sizing called Tag Number (often shown as "No."). Bigger numbers = thinner thread.</li>
      <li>Tex system (often shown as T or tex): Bigger numbers = thicker thread. Why? Tex is calculated as weight in grams of 1,000 meters of thread.</li>
      <li>Decitex system (often shown asdtex): Bigger numbers = thicker thread. Why? Tex is calculated as weight in grams of 10,000 meters of thread.</li>
      <li>Denier system (often shown as Td or d): Bigger numbers = thicker thread. Why? Denier is calculated as weight in grams of 9,000 meters of thread.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.amann-mettler.com/en/products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mettler thread numbering system</a></li>
      <li>and more</li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    In this course we will often use the <a href="https://guetermann.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gütermann</a> brand threads called
    <strong> Mara</strong> or <strong>Tera</strong>. Gütermann is a reliable brand of thread with
    good weight options, many consistent color ranges, and strengths suited to heavy-duty product work.</p>
  <p>
    The Gutermann system is the system we will be using most frequently in this course.
  </p>
  <h3 id="gutermann-thread-tickets">Gütermann thread sizing (aka "tickets")</h3>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img
      src="https://static.robray.net/images/soft-goods/gutermann-mara-70.jpg"
      alt="Gütermann Mara thread spool with ticket number 70 shown on the yellow end label"
      width="800"
      height="533"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Example spool: This is a Gütermann Mara 70 thread spool. The word "Mara" is followed by the ticket number <strong>70</strong> on the cone end.
      You can see the "7" and a bit of the "0" on the yellow end in this photo. This 70 is the Mara weight number.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Gütermann's numbers are their own system (commercial systems are often called "tickets") may feel backwards. In the commercial/ticket system
    used for Gütermann's threads, <strong>the bigger the number, the finer (thinner) the thread</strong>.</p>
    <p>
      For example, Mara 220 is much thinner than Mara 30. Bigger number, thinner thread.
    </p>
    <p>
      Why the word "ticket?" This number used to be printed on a physical paper ticket attached to the spool. Now it is printed on the cone end.
    </p>
  <h3 id="tex-numbers">Tex numbers</h3>
  <p>
    Other threads use the <strong>Tex</strong> system. Tex is kind of the <em>metric system for thread weight</em> and it is an international standard.</p>
    <p>A <em>higher</em> Tex number means a <em>thicker</em> thread.</p>
    <p>Tex = weight in grams of thread per 1,000 meters.
      <p>Here's a couple of Tex examples:<ul>
        <li>Let's say we have a thread that weighs <strong>12 grams</strong> per 1,000 meters. This means the Tex number is <strong>12.</strong></li>
        <li>If the thread weighs <strong>24 grams</strong> per 1,000 meters, the Tex number is <strong>24.</strong></li></ul></p>
        <p>
          The tex number goes up as the thread strand gets heavier.</p>
          </p>
    <p>We will mostly stick to Gutermann's ticket number system for this course as we will be using Gütermann's Mara and Tera in ticket numbers 100 and 70 in this course.</p>
